<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Srinivasa Ramanujan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1887">1887</a>–<a href="/years/1920">1920</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Srinivasa Ramanujan Iyengar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Srinivasa Ramanujan was an Indian mathematician, born in Erode in British-ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, who made substantial contributions to mathematical analysis, number theory, infinite series and continued fractions. Working at first in isolation, he began a correspondence in 1913 with G. H. Hardy at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, who arranged for him to travel there. He independently compiled nearly 3,900 results, most since proven correct, and became one of the youngest Fellows of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-society">Royal Society</a> and the first Indian Fellow of Trinity College, Cambridge. Ill health forced his return to India in 1919, and he died in 1920 at the age of 32; his &quot;lost notebook&quot; was rediscovered in 1976.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/srinivasa-ramanujan.jpg" alt="Srinivasa Ramanujan" width="330" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Srinivasa_Ramanujan-Add._MS_a94_version2_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
